hello friends i am dr umar malik and  welcome to another video from math  simplified  in this video we will talk about  osteoarthritis  osteoarthritis also known as  degenerative joint disease  or degenerative arthritis is the most  common chronic joint condition  worldwide it occurs when the protective  cartilage that cushions the ends of your  bones  wears down over time although  osteoarthritis can damage  any joint the disorder most commonly  affects the joints in your hands  knees hips and spine this video on  osteoarthritis is a part of series of  videos on joint related issues  that we are trying to educate more  people about you can also check out  our other videos in this series like  diet for joint pain  rheumatic arthritis most common causes  of joint pain  etc so if you haven't watched our  previous  videos on this topic click the banner  above or the links in the description  box down below  to watch them after you've watched this  video so without further ado  let's begin with our video a joint is  where two bones come together  the ends of these bones are covered with  protective tissue  called cartilage cartilage is a tough  rubbery substance that's flexible and  softer than the bone  its job is to protect the ends of the  bone within a joint and to allow them to  move easily against  each other which leads to smooth  movement of the joints  and also helps the joints in bearing  your own body weight  with osteoarthritis this cartilage  breaks down  these bone surfaces become pitted and  rough  this can cause pain within the joint and  irritation in the surrounding tissues  damaged cartilage can't repair itself  this is because  cartilage doesn't contain any blood  vessels  when cartilage veers away completely the  cushioning buffer that it provides  decreases allowing for bone to bone  contact  this leads to pain stiffness and other  symptoms associated with osteoarthritis  osteoarthritis occurs most commonly in  older population  although it can occur in adults of any  age and is a leading cause of disability  and affects more than 30 million men  and women in the united states alone  let's begin by understanding how  osteoarthritis develops  well the most common cause of  osteoarthritis is  long-term joint damage and this is the  reason why osteoarthritis most commonly  occurs in  older population with age our joints get  damaged due to various reasons  this damage can accumulate over time the  older you are  the more wear and tear you have had on  your joints  direct joint damage may also lead to  osteoarthritis  this includes factors such as fractures  near the joints  joint dislocations torn cartilages  and ligament injuries other causes that  can lead to osteoarthritis  include joint malformations obesity  poor posture family history and in some  cases  gender females are usually at a higher  risk  due to menopausal changes  osteoarthritis can occur in any joint  however the most commonly affected areas  of the body  include the small joints of the hands  fingertips knees hips and spine  typically as a neck or lower back  the most common symptoms of  osteoarthritis include  pain in the joint which is usually the  presenting symptom  pain is especially more severe after  movements  and fatigue of the joint pain of  osteoarthritis is  also more common when the person wakes  up in the morning  you can feel certain discomfort when  pressing on the area of the joint  with your fingers which is known as  joint tenderness  joints can feel stiff especially in the  morning when you wake up  inflammation can also be present which  basically means your joints become  tender red and swollen as  osteoarthritis becomes more advanced  pain associated with it  becomes more intense over time  swelling in the joint and surrounding  area may also occur  recognizing the early symptoms of  osteoarthritis can help you to better  manage this condition  as the disease progresses in severity  other symptoms may also develop  such as increased swelling and  inflammation of the joint  normally synovial fluid acts as a  lubricant-like barrier  which helps to reduce friction during  movement in the joint  however in osteoarthritis it accumulates  in larger amounts and can cause  joint swelling fragments of broken off  cartilage may also float within the  synovial fluid  causing increased pain and swelling  you can also feel intense pain which  increases over time  you may feel pain during activities but  also when you are at rest you may feel  an increase in your pain level as the  day progresses or  more swelling in your joints if you have  used them a lot throughout the day  you can also experience decreased range  of motion of the joints involved  you may not be able to move the involved  joint easily  due to stiffness or pain in your joints  this can make it harder to do  day to day activities  as the joint continues to wear down  muscle weakness  bone spurs and joint deformity may also  develop  nerve involvement can also occur  especially if your spine is involved  there may be associated neural symptoms  such as weakness  numbness and tingling and shooting pain  depending upon the nerve root involved  the joint damage caused by severe  osteoarthritis  is irreversible but treatment can help  to reduce the symptoms  in one of our previous videos we  discussed about rheumatoid arthritis  although both osteoarthritis and  rheumatoid arthritis  affect the joint and present with pain  and swelling  these are entirely different diseases  osteoarthritis is a degenerative  condition  which basically means that it increases  in severity over time  rheumatide arthritis on the other hand  is an autoimmune disorder  people with rheumatoid arthritis have  immune system  that mistakenly damages the soft lining  around the joints  as the immune system damages the joints  fluid buildup within the joint occurs  causing stiffness pain swelling and  inflammation  to know more about rheumatic arthritis  watch our video by clicking the banner  above  if you're not sure which form of  arthritis you have your best  bet is to talk to your doctor your  doctor can easily diagnose the  appropriate condition  and help you to manage it in a better  way  osteoarthritis is often a slow  developing disease that can be hard to  diagnose  until it starts to cause pain or  debilitating symptoms  although your clinician can diagnose  osteoarthritis  by just examining your body and taking a  detailed history  he may use imaging and other tests to  confirm the diagnosis  in addition to x-rays your doctor may  use an mri scan to diagnose  osteoarthritis  this imaging test uses radio waves and a  magnetic field to create  images of bone and soft tissue  and has the highest diagnostic  capability other diagnostic tests  include a blood test to rule out other  conditions that can cause joint pain  such as rheumatoid arthritis or  infections  a joint fluid analysis can also be used  to determine  whether gout or infection is the  underlying cause of inflammation  osteoarthritis treatment is centered  upon  symptom management the type of treatment  that will help you the most will  largely be determined by the severity of  the symptoms  and their location often lifestyle  changes  over the counter medications and home  remedies will be enough to provide you  with relief from pain  stiffness and swelling at home  treatments and lifestyle changes for  osteoarthritis  include regular exercise weight  management  adequate sleep and heat and cold therapy  for the joints  physical activity strengthens the  muscles around your joints  and helps to relieve stiffness aim for  at least 20 to 30 minutes of physical  movement  at least every other day choose gentle  low impact activities such as walking or  swimming  stretching exercises in yoga can also  improve joint flexibility  and help with pain management  being overweight can put a strain on  your joints and can cause  pain shedding excess pounds helps to  relieve this pressure and reduces pain  a healthy weight can also lower your  risk of other health problems such as  diabetes and heart diseases  resting your muscles can reduce swelling  and inflammation  getting enough sleep at night can also  help you to manage  pain more effectively  you can experiment with heat or cold  therapy to relieve  muscle pain and stiffness apply a cold  or hot compress to sew joints for 15  to 20 minutes several times a day  gentle stretching exercises can be very  helpful for people with osteoarthritis  especially if you have stiffness or pain  in your knees  hips or back stretching can help improve  mobility and  range of motion in the joints as with  any exercise plan check with your doctor  before beginning to make sure it's the  right course of action for you  i will leave links to some of the videos  which specifically talk about  exercises for people with osteoarthritis  in the description box down below  so make sure to check them out  now let's look at the various  medications that are used to treat  osteoarthritis  there are a number of medications that  can help to provide relief from pain  and swelling that occurs in  osteoarthritis they include  oral pain killers like tylenol which  contains  acetaminophen topical painkillers are  also very commonly used  which are over the counter products and  are available as creams  gels and patches they help to numb the  joint area  and can provide pain relief especially  for mild arthritis  non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs  are most commonly used for managing the  pain  and swelling in osteoarthritis these  include drugs such as advil which  contains ibuprofen  a leaf containing naproxen and several  other drugs  antidepressant drugs in low doses are  also used in osteoarthritis  most commonly duloxetine is used which  increases the pain threshold of the  patient  steroids like prednisone are also used  in cases of severe osteoarthritis  this medication is available in oral  form  they may also be given by injecting  directly into the joint  your doctor may also recommend braces  and supports  in case of spine or severe knee  involvement  in some cases surgery such as knee  replacement or  hip replacement may also be required in  case of  very severe disease with this we come to  the end of this video  in our upcoming videos we'll be telling  you how to manage  osteoarthritis naturally if you want to  know the best diet for joint pain  watch our video on the topic by clicking  the banner above  we at med simplified are committed to  bringing you the latest and updated  content  you can support us by spreading the word  and sharing our videos to your friends  and family  also you can become a member of our  youtube channel or support us  on patreon all the links are in the  description below  thank you so much for watching and see  you in the next video
 

